rt the opening of  dialogue between  authorities and the opposition  and there was strong support for role of  the osce  the osce might be seeking ways to  facilitate dialogue in belarus  belarus is member of the osce  it's not so much a mediation but on  opening  communication channels within belarus  finally the demonstrations in belarus  are not against any neighboring country  or entity the demonstrations in belarus  are for the rights of the people of  belarus  this is about the belarusian people and  their legitimate  right to determine the future path  of their country the european union  stands at the side  of the belarusian people violence  has to stop only an inclusive  dialogue will find solutions  the future of belarus must be decided  by the belarusian people in belarus it  must grow from within  thank you thank you madam president  for your message and we'll turn to the q  a session now  we have a lot of people tuning in please  let me state on beforehand could you  please  um state clearly whom you want to  address your question to so that it  makes easier to switch from one side to  the other  we'll start with john chalmers from  reuters  john you have the floor  can you hear us john yes hello yes can  you hear me  yes please yes hi and this is a  question for the uh the president of the  european council  in the recent hours while the uh eu  leaders were meeting uh president shenko  ordered the police  to put down protests in minsk and this  signals an escalation of the situation  there did you take this into account  during your conversations  and what is your response to the latest  developments  our message is very clear stop the  violence  and we don't accept the impunity we  don't tolerate the impunity and that's  why we took this a very  important decision it's our intention to  decide  sanctions targeted extensions in order  to target  the individual's authorities involved  in the process of violence or in the  falsification of the elections 